WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.934
-I have heard this is great.

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:06.200
-No, this is your play.
This is your play.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.634
Yeah, "Gutenberg! The Musical!"
-Yes.

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:10.300
-I want to talk to you
about this.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.868
You were on our show
when we did "Late Night"

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:15.300
and back when you guys
were in "Book of Mormon."

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.901
-"Book of Mormon."
-Yes. Do you remember that?

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:18.400
-Uh-huh.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.534
-You were --
It was the funniest thing

00:00:20.534 --> 00:00:22.434
I've ever seen on Broadway.
-Thank you.

00:00:22.434 --> 00:00:24.267
I go, "Oh, my gosh, this is
the funniest thing ever."

00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.501
-Until now.
-Now you're back.

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:29.467
Now you're back, everybody.
Yeah, until now is right.

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:31.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Do you like that?

00:00:31.834 --> 00:00:33.534
This is our big reunion
on Broadway.

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:34.767
-I was waiting.

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.334
I was like, "What are these guys
gonna do together?

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:37.501
They have to do --"

00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:39.367
Your chemistry was so great.

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:42.033
I go -- And I have a great idea,
I think.

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:44.634
I want you to do
a detective show on TV.

00:00:44.634 --> 00:00:45.968
-Oh.
-That doesn't feel like

00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:47.701
something either of us
wants to do.

00:00:47.701 --> 00:00:49.367
-Yeah.
No, this is great.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:50.634
This is what you have to do.
-Okay.

00:00:50.634 --> 00:00:52.200
-And it's like a "Matlock"
type of thing.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.367
-Oh, okay.
-Alright. Tell me more.

00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:55.000
-Oh, see, I was going
"Murder, She Wrote."

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.033
-Yeah, that's what makes it fun.

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.033
You can do "Murder, She Wrote."
You can do "Matlock."

00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:01.467
-So this is sort of
like a hybrid.

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:03.968
Do you want us in the same show
here, or is this -- Okay.

00:01:03.968 --> 00:01:06.567
-Yes. I want to see you
in the same show.

00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:08.701
-What about like
a "Simon &amp; Simon"?

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.901
-That makes more sense.
That makes more sense.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:12.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:14.000
"Simon &amp; Simon,"
you can do that, as well.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.300
But I want to see that.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.634
I think that would be
kind of fun to watch.

00:01:16.634 --> 00:01:18.601
-Alright, yeah, it sounds
like your audience

00:01:18.601 --> 00:01:21.100
is really excited
about that one, too.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.000
It feels like
this could be a hit.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.267
-In the meantime,

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:26.567
if you don't have
the detective show,

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:27.734
you have
"Gutenberg! The Musical!"

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:29.067
-We have
"Gutenberg! The Musical!"

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.834
-You can both sing and dance.
You're very talented.

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:33.200
-He can sing and dance
really well.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.601
-You can also sing and dance.

00:01:34.601 --> 00:01:37.033
You know, it's been a long time

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:38.200
since we've done
a show together.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.367
-Do you remember first meeting?

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:41.234
What were your first impressions
of each other?

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.067
-Didn't like the guy very much.
-No, no, no.

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:44.767
We can edit this out.
We can edit all this out.

00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:46.334
-Oh, Andrew.
-Oh, great. Me.

00:01:46.334 --> 00:01:48.667
-We met June 2010.

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:52.400
-Look at this guy.
Dear diary.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.634
-That was so sweet.

00:01:53.634 --> 00:01:56.033
-They flew him to L.A.

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:57.934
And we did a -- we did a --

00:01:57.934 --> 00:02:00.400
-I can do this for hours.
I love this.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.000
-Well, this is going to be
on our detective show.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.400
This is how we hold each other.
-Ooh! I'm excited.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.133
I'm telling you, Peacock.
-So, Andrew came in.

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:07.968
Andrew -- Peacock.
-Yeah.

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:09.534
-Andrew came in,

00:02:09.534 --> 00:02:12.000
and I had done workshops
with other people

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.634
like three or four times,

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:16.367
and then they brought
two celebrities up against --

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:18.701
stacked him
against two big celebs.

00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:20.200
And then he came in last.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.400
And we did the scene together.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.734
It was Cunningham and Price.

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:25.767
And I looked at Trey Parker,
Matt Stone,

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:27.501
and our producer,
Anne Garefino, after,

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:28.801
and I said, "That's the guy."

00:02:28.801 --> 00:02:30.367
And they said, "We agree.
Why do you think that way?"

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:32.200
And I said,
"Because it's the first time

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.367
I've ever been intimidated
by an Elder Price."

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:37.067
-Josh.
-And he was incredible.

00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:38.434
He was absolutely incredible.

00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:40.634
-Was that part of the secret?
-I threatened him,

00:02:40.634 --> 00:02:43.000
which was part
of the temptation factor.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.267
No, it was very --
it was very intimidating.

00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:46.667
And I remember very clearly,

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.033
you know, obviously,
getting to read.

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:50.767
But we didn't have any time
together before.

00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:52.434
We just, like,
walked into a room

00:02:52.434 --> 00:02:54.334
and then had to start
reading together.

00:02:54.334 --> 00:02:55.968
So it was, like --
it was very intimidating.

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:57.567
But the two guys
who went in before me

00:02:57.567 --> 00:03:00.100
were pretty damn famous.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:01.400
-Did you see them
leave the room?

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.801
-Yeah, 'cause we were all there
at the same time.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:05.067
-I don't like that.
-But I figured --

00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:06.367
I was like, "I don't
really have a shot."

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:08.501
-Suffice it to say Al Pacino
did not get the role.

00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:11.267
-Oh, no, don't say his name.
Don't say his name.

00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:12.334
-But I figured -- I was like,

00:03:12.334 --> 00:03:13.767
"I don't have a shot
at this at all."

00:03:13.767 --> 00:03:15.834
And then one of the guys
walked out and he looked at me

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:18.167
and he said,
"Musicals are hard."

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:19.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:21.501
-But he said it like this.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.234
[ As Pacino ]
Musicals are hard.

00:03:23.234 --> 00:03:25.200
-Oh, wow. It's Al Pacino.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.400
-[ Normal voice ] I was like,
"Albert, calm down, man."

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.868
-[ As Pacino ]
Musicals are hard.

00:03:28.868 --> 00:03:30.334
-[ As Pacino ]
I don't like musicals.

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:31.634
-Musicals are hard.

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:34.033
-If you want to do
a detective show, I'm your guy.

00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:35.234
-[ Normal voice ]
Oh, wait. No.

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:36.400
-Very good, right?

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.968
-You guys should do the show.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:41.400
But let's talk about
"Gutenberg! The Musical!"

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.734
James Earl Jones Theatre
right now.

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:46.133
And this is something --

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:48.934
People have called it the
"Hamilton" of its generation.

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:50.200
-[ Normal voice ]
Well, that's me.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.200
I'm the person.
-Yeah.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.634
I don't think anyone else
is saying that.

00:03:53.634 --> 00:03:56.067
-Even though technically,
"Hamilton" is the "Hamilton"

00:03:56.067 --> 00:03:58.300
of our generation.
-Right now it is, yeah.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:01.133
-I think this is the "Hamilton"
of its generation.

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:02.400
-You said that.
-Yes.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.901
-I think this is the "Cats"
of this generation.

00:04:03.901 --> 00:04:07.200
-It's a slightly
different premise

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.267
than "Cats" or "Hamilton."

00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:09.734
-It's "Cats"
by way of "Hamilton"

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.767
with two would-be detectives.

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:12.934
-"Wicked."

00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:15.400
You like "Wicked,"
you'll love this.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.634
-If you guys love musicals,
this is for you.

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:20.934
-This is for you.
-This is the one.

00:04:20.934 --> 00:04:24.501
-"Gutenberg"
with these two guys.

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:28.100
In the show, you actually --
you wear a lot of hats.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.334
-We do.
-You play every character

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:31.901
in the whole show.
-Yes.

00:04:31.901 --> 00:04:34.267
-So there are about 120 hats...

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:36.300
-That's me.
That's Andrew.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.667
-That's right.

00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:39.434
-...that we wear
throughout the show

00:04:39.434 --> 00:04:40.868
to sort of differentiate

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:42.501
these different characters
that we play.

00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:44.701
There's about --
-There's over 120 hats.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:46.601
-There's over 120 hats.
-Yeah.

00:04:46.601 --> 00:04:48.734
-And, by the way,
I found out about the hats

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:50.100
after I signed up.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.234
I would not have done a show
with hats had I known,

00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:54.801
'cause there are two things
that scare me --

00:04:54.801 --> 00:04:56.234
hats and choreography.

00:04:56.234 --> 00:04:59.100
The show has both.
-This is a nightmare then, yeah.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.634
-But to be clear,

00:05:00.634 --> 00:05:03.200
we had done two readings
of this musical

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.634
before we, you know,
were really going to do it

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:06.968
in earnest here on Broadway.

00:05:06.968 --> 00:05:09.801
And Josh claims
that he had no idea

00:05:09.801 --> 00:05:11.167
that there were hats involved,

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:14.701
except there is a mention
of hats all over the script.

00:05:14.701 --> 00:05:15.601
-I don't read stage directions.

00:05:15.601 --> 00:05:17.367
-He doesn't read
stage directions.

00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:18.701
-You don't read the script.

00:05:18.701 --> 00:05:20.968
-My character never says,
"I'm wearing a hat right now."

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:23.701
-He just sort of scanned
for his character's name.

00:05:23.701 --> 00:05:25.200
-So I wouldn't know,
so I wouldn't think.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.501
-You wouldn't know.

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:28.934
But what happens when someone
grabs the wrong hat?

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:30.234
-Oh, this happened
the other night.

00:05:30.234 --> 00:05:32.400
-It happens.
Sometimes the hats fall off.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.834
Sometimes, you know --

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:37.901
-This past week, we've had a hat
fly into the audience.

00:05:37.901 --> 00:05:39.234
There was a day where --

00:05:39.234 --> 00:05:41.100
Do any of you watch
"Only Murders in the Building"?

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.033
You know that scene -- Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:43.033 --> 00:05:44.968
-One of the best
detective shows...

00:05:44.968 --> 00:05:46.300
-One of the best detective shows
of all time.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:47.701
-...on television right now.

00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:49.100
That's what people are watching.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:50.434
-It's a weird Jimmy obsession
right now.

00:05:50.434 --> 00:05:52.234
But, yes, this detective show.

00:05:52.234 --> 00:05:55.968
-America wants you guys
to do the detective show

00:05:55.968 --> 00:05:57.300
"Only Murders in the Building."

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.133
You guys love
"Only Murders in the Building"?

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:01.167
How about with these guys?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:01.167 --> 00:06:04.067
-So, like, Steve Martin,
I went to the white room

00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:05.901
and I found myself holding a hat

00:06:05.901 --> 00:06:07.934
I've never held
in my entire life.

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:09.834
And I looked at Andrew onstage,

00:06:09.834 --> 00:06:11.400
and this is
my version of improv.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.534
I go, "Andrew, what the hell
am I doing with this hat?"

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:16.033
I had, like, no --
I had no improv for it.

00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:17.434
-There's no game face.
-No, there's no game face.

00:06:17.434 --> 00:06:19.267
-That's how you improvised?
-Yeah. I go, "What do I do now?"

00:06:19.267 --> 00:06:21.200
Or he'll say, "I wasn't
supposed to say that,"

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.868
or, "You weren't
supposed to say that,"

00:06:22.868 --> 00:06:24.167
or, "Where am I?"
-You just call him out?

00:06:24.167 --> 00:06:26.367
-Sort of just, like,
blunt-force improv.

00:06:26.367 --> 00:06:28.267
So I said this to him,
and he goes,

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:30.601
"No, put that back
where you found it."

00:06:30.601 --> 00:06:32.300
[ Laughter ]

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:33.501
-We got back on track.

00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:34.801
-Of course you
get back on track.

00:06:34.801 --> 00:06:36.067
-It's live theater, Jimmy.

00:06:36.067 --> 00:06:39.434
-I was very lucky to meet
your mom and dad

00:06:39.434 --> 00:06:40.601
and your brother backstage.

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:41.868
-They were very lucky
to meet you.

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:44.467
-No, come on.
But you know what's funny?

00:06:44.467 --> 00:06:47.434
We were talking, and you guys
were nice enough to come on

00:06:47.434 --> 00:06:49.834
when I hosted "Late Night"
back in the day.

00:06:49.834 --> 00:06:51.901
This is us here, in fact.

00:06:51.901 --> 00:06:53.934
-Oh, look at this.
-2012.

00:06:53.934 --> 00:06:55.701
-That's me.
That's Andrew.

00:06:55.701 --> 00:06:58.400
-No, that's is -- that's you.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.667
And your mom said that it was
one of her favorite --

00:07:01.667 --> 00:07:03.501
-My mom. I have done --

00:07:03.501 --> 00:07:05.801
I have done a bunch
of late night since that.

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:08.801
For some reason --
Probably 2,500.

00:07:08.801 --> 00:07:11.868
And for some reason,
my mother always says to me,

00:07:11.868 --> 00:07:13.701
"I'll never forget the time

00:07:13.701 --> 00:07:17.267
you and Andrew sang
'Take On Me' with Jimmy Fallon.

00:07:17.267 --> 00:07:19.167
It was my favorite."
-That's right.

00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:21.267
-That's right, Mom. Mom is --
-Susan Gad's here.

00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:22.434
-My mom is here. Susan.

00:07:22.434 --> 00:07:24.334
-Susan Gad Schwartz is here.

00:07:24.334 --> 00:07:25.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:25.534 --> 00:07:27.501
Aw, look at Mom and Dad.
-Look at that. Come on.

00:07:27.501 --> 00:07:29.501
-Mom and Dad.
-She has great taste.

00:07:29.501 --> 00:07:31.167
-Oh, Mama.

00:07:31.167 --> 00:07:34.367
-But I thought it'd be kind of
fun, if you wouldn't mind,

00:07:34.367 --> 00:07:35.968
to maybe re--

00:07:35.968 --> 00:07:37.601
-Should we do it?
-I mean, sure.

00:07:37.601 --> 00:07:38.868
-I think it's time.

00:07:38.868 --> 00:07:40.834
Mom, this is for you.
-This is for Susan.

00:07:40.834 --> 00:07:42.501
-This is for you, Susan.

00:07:42.501 --> 00:07:45.200
[ "Take On Me" plays ]

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.167
-Oh, yeah.

00:07:47.167 --> 00:07:48.968
Do you remember this, Jimmy?

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:51.534
-♪ Talking away ♪

00:07:51.534 --> 00:07:54.300
♪ I don't know what I'm to say ♪

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.167
♪ I'll say it anyway ♪

00:07:57.167 --> 00:08:00.701
♪ Today is another day
to find you ♪

00:08:00.701 --> 00:08:03.601
♪ Shyin' away ♪

00:08:03.601 --> 00:08:06.200
♪ I'll be comin'
for your love, okay ♪

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:10.534
-♪ Take on me ♪

00:08:10.601 --> 00:08:12.133
-♪ Take on me ♪

00:08:12.133 --> 00:08:16.334
-♪ Take me on ♪

00:08:16.334 --> 00:08:18.000
-♪ Take on me ♪

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.200
-♪ I'll be gone ♪

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.667
-This one's for you, Mama.

00:08:22.667 --> 00:08:28.934
♪ In a day or two ♪

00:08:28.934 --> 00:08:31.334
-Josh Gad and Andrew Rannells.

00:08:31.334 --> 00:08:33.834
"Gutenberg! The Musical!"
is on Broadway

00:08:33.834 --> 00:08:37.534
at the James Earl Jones Theatre
through January 28th.

00:08:37.534 --> 00:08:39.567
We're playing a new game
with them after the break.

00:08:39.567 --> 00:08:41.200
Come on back, everybody.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:44.434
♪ Take on me ♪

00:08:44.434 --> 00:08:46.901
-I love you, Mama.

00:08:46.901 --> 00:08:51.200
-♪ Take me on ♪

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:52.734
-♪ Take on me ♪

00:08:52.734 --> 00:08:56.467
-♪ I'll be gone ♪

